{
  // **This file is the self-hosting default. The official deployment does not use it.**
  //
  // It deliberately carries no account-specific values: no database id, no namespace id, no
  // route, no vars. Fill them in for your own deployment and `git pull` will never conflict
  // here, because upstream has no reason to touch those lines.
  //
  // The official tidemarks.io deployment generates its own configuration at build time from
  // Workers Builds' build variables — see `scripts/deploy.ts` and `docs/deployment.md`.
  //
  // Two things worth knowing before trying to avoid the generator (both checked against
  // wrangler 4.124, not inferred from the docs):
  //
  //  - Wrangler does not interpolate `${VAR}` in a configuration file.
  //  - `wrangler deploy` has no flag that overrides a D1 `database_id`, an R2 `bucket_name`
  //    or a KV `id`, so a command line cannot stand in for them either.
  //
  // Leaving an id out entirely is also not a way round it. Wrangler will provision the
  // resource and write the id back — which works from a laptop and does not work from a build
  // environment, because that environment cannot commit to the repository. The id is then
  // lost, and every later deploy tries to create the resource again and fails with "already
  // exists" (code 10014). This project has been there; see docs/deployment.md.
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "tidemarks",
  "main": "worker/index.ts",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-07-01",
  // Stated rather than left at wrangler's 8787: the Vite dev server proxies /api and /auth to
  // this port, and that target is written in vite.config.ts. The two have to agree, so the
  // number belongs in a file rather than in whatever each of us types.
  "dev": {
    "port": 5002
  },
  "assets": {
    "directory": "./dist",
    "binding": "ASSETS",
    "not_found_handling": "single-page-application",
    // Everything the Worker owns has to be listed here. `single-page-application` means an
    // unmatched path serves index.html, so a missing entry does not 404 — it answers /mcp
    // with the React app, and the failure reads as "this MCP server speaks HTML".
    "run_worker_first": ["/api/*", "/auth/*", "/mcp", "/authorize", "/oauth/*", "/.well-known/*"]
  },

  // No `routes`. Without one the Worker is served from `<name>.workers.dev`, which is what
  // makes a fresh deployment usable before anybody has bought a domain. Add a `routes` entry
  // with `"custom_domain": true` once you have a hostname whose zone is on your account.

  "d1_databases": [
    {
      "binding": "DB",
      "database_name": "tidemarks",
      // "database_id": "…" — from `npx wrangler d1 create tidemarks`. Required; read the note
      // at the top of this file before leaving it out.
      //
      // `migrations_dir` is stated rather than left to the default so a reader can find where
      // the schema lives. `npm run deploy` applies these before uploading the Worker, which is
      // the order that matters: a Worker reading a column the database has not got yet fails
      // on the first request, not at deploy time.
      "migrations_dir": "./migrations"
    }
  ],
  "r2_buckets": [
    {
      "binding": "BUCKET",
      "bucket_name": "tidemarks"
    }
  ],
  // OAuth grants, authorization codes and access tokens for the MCP server. KV rather than D1
  // because workers-oauth-provider owns the storage and only speaks KV; nothing in here is a
  // reader's data, and every entry expires on its own.
  "kv_namespaces": [
    {
      "binding": "OAUTH_KV"
      // "id": "…" — from `npx wrangler kv namespace create OAUTH_KV`. This is the binding the
      // 10014 story at the top of this file is about.
    }
  ]

  // No `vars`, and that is not an oversight. Three values belong here and none of them has a
  // default that is right for anybody else:
  //
  //   RP_ID      the hostname passkeys are scoped to. **Permanently locked once the first
  //              passkey is registered** — changing it invalidates every passkey.
  //   ORIGIN     "https://" + that hostname.
  //   MAIL_FROM  sender of the magic-code mail, on a domain Resend has verified. **Not
  //              necessarily the hostname above** — Resend only knows about the domain you
  //              verified with it, and refuses every message from any other.
  //
  // A plausible-looking placeholder is worse than nothing here: two of the three fail
  // silently. A deployment with somebody else's RP_ID deploys fine and cannot be logged into,
  // and one with an unverified MAIL_FROM deploys fine and mails nothing.
  //
  // Leaving MAIL_FROM unset is a supported configuration, not a broken one: without
  // RESEND_API_KEY the login code is written to the log (`npx wrangler tail`) instead of
  // mailed, which is the whole zero-vendor self-hosting path. See worker/email.ts.
  //
  // Whether RP_ID should fall back to the request's host is an open question — issue #9.
  //
  // "OPEN_SIGNUP": "true" — absent, and absent means only addresses in the `signup_allowlist`
  // table can create an account. Adding this line is the launch line itself (ADR-0004): the
  // moment data starts belonging to people who did not agree it could be thrown away. It is a
  // deploy on purpose, so that opening signup leaves a commit behind, while adding one friend
  // to the allowlist is a `wrangler d1 execute` and needs nothing.

  // secrets (npx wrangler secret put): COOKIE_SECRET, RESEND_API_KEY.
  // COOKIE_SECRET is required — the Worker deploys without it and then nobody can log in.
  // RESEND_API_KEY unset means magic codes go to the log instead of the mail, as above.
}
